  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 321 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA frontal boundary extending from the Texas Hill Country toward \nthe Great Lakes is the focus for ongoing shower activity early\nthis morning. Ascent on the flank of the subtropical jet stream\nwill continue to support this activity today into tomorrow, while\nan approaching easterly wave is spreading a deeper surge of\ntropical moisture into the Gulf Coast region from the south. As\nthe moisture spreads north, rain chances go up. Heaviest rain may\nstill be closest to the coast. Regional average rainfall amounts\nthrough the weekend are expected to be around 0.75-1.0 inches in \nthe Delta to around 2.0-3.0 inches in the Pine Belt. Upper-end\nrainfall amounts could be up to around 3 inches if training\nrainfall adds up for an area, with isolated amounts of 6 to 7\ninches primarily farther south and east outside of our forecast\narea. With dry existing conditions in place, the threat for\nwidespread flash flooding is low. Heavy rain over urban areas or \nother flood-prone areas would cause an isolated risk for any \nflooding.\n\nBy Monday, a surging polar cold front will sweep into the Mid- \nSouth and Gulf Coast. A breezy and cooling north wind will persist\nthrough the day Monday and chase the decreasing rain chances. The\nupper-level trough configuration won't fully clear rain chances\nfrom the Gulf Coast and Pine Belt, but the threat for heavy rain\nshould end. A 1025mb surface high moving from the Canadian \nPrairies toward the Mid-Atlantic is around the 90th percentile in\nstrength for this time of year. We won't receive the full force \nof this high, but cooler temps will still be felt. Low \ntemperatures Monday night and onward look to range from the 50s to\nlow 60s. These values would be about 5-15 degrees below normal \nfor early October. The late week period is uncertain, with the\npattern ultimately determining if dry air remains entrenched or \nif warm and moist air can lift back into the region. The official \nforecast leans into a gradual warming trend with low end POPs in \nthe south. /NF/",
